emscripten-core/emscripten @ 887305e6 (main, 2026-09-26, emscripten-version.txt
6.0.11-git): six hand-written heap indexings use a signed shift, which is wrong
above 2 GiB under CAN_ADDRESS_2GB.

Proposal only. picovoxel, an unofficial community TypeScript/WebAssembly binding
of LEAP 71's PicoGK, does not use these GL bindings. It hit the same bug class
in its own glue code and found these stragglers in the library while fixing it.

The rule emscripten already follows: getHeapOffset (src/parseTools.mjs:412-425)
emits `>>>` instead of `>>` when CAN_ADDRESS_2GB is set, and CAN_ADDRESS_2GB is
set automatically whenever MAXIMUM_MEMORY > 2 GiB without MEMORY64
(tools/link.py:1668-1669). Every access written through the macro is correct.

The defect: six accesses in the WebGL libraries are written by hand and bypass
the macro, so they keep the signed shift under every setting. A pointer at or
past 2 GiB is >= 2**31; JS `>>` coerces to int32 first, so `p >> 2` goes
negative and the index reads the wrong element (for the subarray/set family it
clamps without an error). The neighbouring glUniformMatrix4x3fv in
libwebgl2.js uses `{{{ getHeapOffset('value', 'float') }}}`; the two idioms sit
five lines apart.

  src/lib/libwebgl.js:3052,3061,3070,3079  glVertexAttrib{1,2,3,4}fv
  src/lib/libwebgl2.js:890,898             glVertexAttribI4{i,ui}v

The fix is `>>` -> `>>>` at those sites. Output values: unchanged below 2 GiB;
correct above it. Routing the sites through getHeapOffset would also cover
MEMORY64, where the macro divides instead of shifting; this patch keeps to the
minimum change and leaves that choice to the maintainers.

How picovoxel met the bug class (2026-07-27): its own glue read a 120 MB mesh
staging buffer as HEAPU32.subarray(pointer >> 2, ...). When the allocator placed
the buffer above 2 GiB, subarray clamped the negative start instead of throwing,
so the read returned a correctly sized window about 1.4 GiB away, and element
count, byte count and volume all still matched. That was picovoxel's bug and is
fixed there; these six lines are the library's share of the same class.

A regex scan of both files for HEAP*[... >> n] without `>>>` finds exactly
these six lines; other src/lib/*.js files were not scanned.

Target file sha256 (emscripten main @ 887305e67c418ce26cdf20ea60bc78777f2b0b8f):
